<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Fei River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/383">383</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Fei River was fought in 383 in present-day Anhui, where a much smaller Eastern Jin army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-xuan">Xie Xuan</a>, directed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-an">Xie An</a>, routed the invading force of Fu Jian of the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. The defeat shattered Fu Jian's empire, which had unified northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: subject peoples rebelled and founded Later Yan, Later Qin, Western Yan and other states, while the south remained independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river.jpg" alt="Battle of Fei River" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Fei_River.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Under Fu Jian the Di-led Former Qin conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> in 370, took modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> from the Jin in 373 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> in 379, and by 381 held all of north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After a Jin attempt to retake Xiangyang failed in May 383, Fu Jian conscripted six of every ten able-bodied men. His brother Fu Rong, who had opposed the campaign, led an advance force of 300,000, and Fu Jian set out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> with 270,000 cavalry and 600,000 infantry. Many of these troops, levied from conquered peoples, had little loyalty to the Former Qin and little training.</p><p>Emperor Xiaowu of Jin gave the Huai front to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-shi">Xie Shi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-xuan">Xie Xuan</a> with the 80,000-strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beifu-army">Beifu Army</a>, whose officers brought their own hereditary troops and who were trained as a professional force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In October 383 Fu Rong took the Jin city of Shouyang, and Fu Jian hurried ahead with 8,000 light cavalry. He sent the captured Jin official Zhu Xu to urge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-shi">Xie Shi</a> to surrender, but Zhu told him the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> army had not yet fully arrived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-xuan">Xie Xuan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-laozhi">Liu Laozhi</a> then attacked the advance force with 5,000 men and killed 15,000. In November the armies faced each other across the Fei River. Xie Xuan asked the Qin to draw back so that the Jin could cross; Fu Jian agreed, meaning to strike them in midstream. As the Qin pulled back, Zhu Xu shouted that the Qin army was defeated, panic spread, Fu Rong was killed when his horse fell, and the whole army collapsed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>An estimated 70 to 80 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> troops died in the fighting and the flight, from combat, hunger and exposure, while Jin losses were minor. Fu Jian withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-chui">Murong Chui</a>, who rebelled in early 384. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> fell to the Western Yan in 385, and Fu Jian was killed that year by his former general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-chang">Yao Chang</a>. The Jin advanced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a>, and the south stayed independent of the north until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a> in 589.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Fei River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Fu Jian of the Former Qin invaded with a vast army in 383, Xie Shi and Xie Xuan, under the overall direction of Xie An, defeated its advance forces at the <b>Battle of Fei River</b>, and the Former Qin collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Fu Jiān's bid to unify China broke at the <b>Battle of Fei River</b>, Tuoba Gui, son or grandson of the last Prince of Dai, reasserted Tuoba independence in 386, first as Prince of Dai and then as Prince of Wei, whence the state's name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Fei River</b> battle he fled to the Eastern Jin.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-liang">Former Liang</a></li><li><blockquote>Fu Jian's invasion of the south ended in rout at the <b>Fei River</b> in 383 against the Eastern Jin.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning east with the monk Kumarajiva and the spoils, he learned of the <b>Fei River</b> defeat, seized Guzang (Wuwei) in 386 and made himself ruler of the Hexi Corridor, later taking the title Heavenly King of Liang.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-liang-386-403">Later Liang (386-403)</a></li><li><blockquote>Yao Chang, a Qiang general of Former Qin, rebelled after the <b>Fei River</b> defeat in 383, captured and killed the Qin ruler Fu Jian in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor of Qin in 386, with his capital at Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a></li></ul></section>
